#### Article 16

The Regional Gender Mainstreaming Working Group as referred to in Article 15 paragraph (1) has the following duties: a. promoting and facilitating Gender Mainstreaming to each Regional Apparatus;  
b. conducting socialization and advocacy of Gender Mainstreaming to Sub-District Heads and Wali Nagari;  
c. preparing an annual work program; d. encouraging the realization of gender-responsive planning and budgeting;  
e. preparing an annual work plan for the Gender Mainstreaming working group;  
f. being responsible to the Regent through the Vice Regent; g. formulating Gender Mainstreaming policy recommendations to the Regent;  
h. preparing a Regional Gender profile;  
i. monitoring the implementation of Gender Mainstreaming in each Regional Apparatus.  j. establishing a technical team to conduct analysis of gender-responsive regional budgets;  
k. preparing a regional action plan on Gender Mainstreaming in the region; andl. encouraging the implementation of selection and appointment of focal points in each Regional Apparatus.

#### Article 17

1. The technical team as referred to in Article 16 letter j consists of officials who understand gender-responsive budget analysis.  
2. The Regional Gender Mainstreaming regional action plan as referred to in Article 16 letter k contains: a. gender mainstreaming in regional legislation;  
b. gender mainstreaming in the regional development cycle;  
c. strengthening of Gender Mainstreaming institutions in the region; andd. strengthening community participation in the region

#### Article 18

1. The Gender Mainstreaming Focal Point in each Regional Apparatus as referred to in Article 16 letter l consists of officials and/or staff in charge of planning and/or program duties (2) The Gender Mainstreaming Focal Point as referred to in paragraph (1), has the following duties:a. promoting Gender Mainstreaming in the work unit;  
b. facilitating the preparation of work plans and budgeting of Regional Apparatus that are gender-responsive; c. conducting training, socialization, advocacy of Gender Mainstreaming to all officials and staff within the Regional Apparatus;  
d. reporting the implementation of Gender Mainstreaming to the leadership of the Regional Apparatus;  
e. encouraging the implementation of gender analysis on policies, programs, and activities in the work unit; andf. facilitating the preparation of gender data in each Regional Apparatus.  
3. The Gender Mainstreaming Focal Point as referred to in paragraph (1), is selected and appointed by the leadership of the Regional Apparatus

## Part Four

Increasing the Role, Quality, and Capacity of Women in the Political Field

#### Article 19

Efforts to increase the role, quality and capacity of women in the political field include:
a. conducting socialization to increase women's political participation in general elections; b. implementing programs that encourage the level of women's involvement in political processes and public positions;  
c. facilitating women's self-development in organizing, associating and assembling;  
d. involving women in decision-making concerning women's interests;

## Part Five

Increasing the Role, Quality, and Capacity of Women in the Legal Field

#### Article 20

Efforts to increase the role, quality and capacity of women in the legal field include: a. conducting socialization to increase women's legal knowledge and awareness; b. mapping and reviewing gender-biased policies; c. involving women's organizations in the process of drafting and discussing Regional legal products related to women's participation;

## Part Six

Increasing the Role, Quality, and Capacity of Women in the Social Field

#### Article 21

Efforts to increase the role, quality and capacity of women in the social field include:
a. increasing knowledge and skills to encourage the fulfillment of education according to their potential;  
b. increasing and fulfilling the quality of health for women;  
c. increasing awareness and knowledge about independent, healthy and prosperous family planning including access to consultation services and marriage registration;  
d. increasing women's awareness of the impact of early marriage;  
e. facilitating and seeking the preservation of customs and the development of science, technology, arts and culture for the advancement of women;  
f. increasing women's awareness and knowledge of independent, healthy and prosperous family planning;  
g. implementing programs to reduce maternal mortality rates;  
h. encouraging the formation of women's organizations in the Region;

## Part Seven

Increasing the Role, Quality, and Capacity of Women in the Economic Field

#### Article 22

Efforts to increase the role, quality and capacity of women in the field include:
a. implementing programs, activities, training to increase women's skills and expertise in order to realize the improvement of women's welfare;  
b. facilitating capital strengthening for women with weak economies;  
c. marketing products from businesses of women with weak economies;  
d. training to develop women's capacity through economic empowerment of pre-prosperous families; e. providing comprehensive entrepreneurship training to women.

# CHAPTER IV

PROTECTION OF WOMEN AND CHILDRENPart One Prevention of Violence against Women and Children

#### Article 23

1. The responsibility of the Regional Government to prevent acts of violence against women and children includes:a. conducting socialization regarding the prevention of violence against women and children by optimizing the role of customary, cultural, religious institutions and community organizations;  
b. providing guidelines on handling acts of violence against women and children; c. involving community participation in recovery and reintegration;  
d. developing an integrated database and information system on acts of violence against women and children;  
e. developing family empowerment programs;  
f. strengthening the function of community organizations working in the field of women's empowerment and child protection; andg. providing public facilities that are comfortable and safe for women and children.  
2. Prevention of acts of violence against women and children as referred to in paragraph (1), is carried out by Regional Apparatus whose duties and functions are in the field of: a. social affairs;  
b. health;  
c. education;  
d. manpower;  
e. women's empowerment and child protection; f. mental and spiritual;  
g. informatics and communication; and h. peace and order.  
3. Prevention as referred to in paragraph (1), is carried out by Regional Apparatus in accordance with their respective fields of duty in an integrated and continuous manner based on the Regional Action Plan.

#### Article 24

The responsibility of the community to prevent acts of violence against women and Children includes: a. monitoring various conditions occurring in their environment that may cause violations of the rights of women and children;  
b. increasing awareness of violations of the rights of women and children;  
c. strengthening the role of community, religious organizations and the family environment; andd. preventing unregistered marriages and marriages at child age.

#### Article 25

The responsibility of parents and families to prevent acts of violence against women and children includes:
a. protecting women and children from various violations of rights within the household;  
b. encouraging the formation of harmonious, educative and democratic families;  
c. providing good examples to children according to religious and customary teachings;  
d. supervising children in their activities inside or outside the home; ande. providing opportunities for children to engage in dialogue and express opinions democratically.

