#### Article 26

1. In order to implement the Protection of Women and Children in the Region, the Regional Government shall establish UPTD PPA in accordance with the provisions of laws and regulations.  
2. UPTD PPA as referred to in paragraph (1) functions to provide services to women and Children who experience criminal and/or civil problems.  
3. The scope of services of UPTD PPA as referred to in paragraph (2) includes:a. complaint services;  
b. health rehabilitation;  
c. social rehabilitation;  
d. assistance;  
e. law enforcement and legal aid; and f. repatriation and social reintegration.  
4. In carrying out its functions, UPTD PPA is obliged to: a. provide services and handling as quickly as possible to women and children who experience problems;  
b. provide convenience, comfort, safety and free of charge to women and children who experience problems;  
c. maintain the confidentiality of women and children who experience problems;  
d. guarantee justice and legal certainty for women and children who experience problems.  
5. UPTD PPA is responsible to the Regional Apparatus that administers governmental affairs in the field of Women's Empowerment and Child Protection.

#### Article 27

1. The implementation of UPTD PPA services is integrative, which is carried out under one roof or networked to provide optimal services to women and children who experience problems.  
2. In the event that the implementation of UPTD PPA services as referred to in paragraph (1) is carried out under one roof, UPTD PPA is responsible for carrying out the entire process in one unit to provide the services needed by women and children who experience problems.  
3. In the event that the implementation of UPTD PPA services as referred to in paragraph (1) is carried out in a networked manner, UPTD PPA is responsible for the entire referral process for services needed by women and children who experience problems.  
4. Every element within the UPTD PPA environment in carrying out its duties must apply the principles of coordination, integration, synchronization, accountability and openness, both within the UPTD PPA environment, as well as in relations between Regional Apparatuses, related agencies, and community elements.

#### Article 28

Further provisions regarding the organization and working procedures of UPTD PPA as referred to in Article 26 and Article 27 shall be stipulated by Regent Regulation in accordance with the provisions of laws and regulations.

### Paragraph 2

Complaint Services

#### Article 29

Complaint services as referred to in Article 26 paragraph (3) letter a may be coordinated by UPTD PPA with related agencies consisting of:
a. hospitals;  
b. community health centers;  
c. integrated service centers for women's empowerment and children d. trauma center shelters;  
d. women crisis centres;  
e. advisory boards for marriage guidance and preservation; f. police;  
g. prosecutors;  
h. courts;  
i. ministry of religion; and/or j. legal aid organizations;

#### Article 30

1. Reports of acts of violence against women and children may be made by reporters consisting of victims, victims' families, the community and/or community organizations in the field of protection of women and children.  
2. Reporters as referred to in paragraph (1) may make complaints:a. directly;  
b. by telephone; and/orc. by letter.  
3. Further provisions regarding the procedures for handling complaint services as referred to in paragraph (1) and paragraph (2) shall be regulated by Regent Regulation.

### Paragraph 3

Health Rehabilitation

#### Article 31

1. Health rehabilitation as referred to in Article 26 paragraph (3) letter b constitutes efforts that include aspects of: a. promotive, improving so that health status increases;  
b. preventive, preventing from contracting diseases; c. curative, curing a person from illness physically and psychologically;  
d. rehabilitative, maintaining so that a person who has not fully recovered can return to being fit as before.  
2. Health rehabilitation as referred to in paragraph (1) is carried out comprehensively, can be accessed 24 (twenty-four) hours, is of quality and is carried out in accordance with health service standards.  
3. Women and Children victims of acts of violence have the right to health services free of charge, including: a. medical services;  
b. medicolegal services;  
c. psychosocial counseling; andd. Visum et Repertum.  
4. Further provisions regarding the procedures for implementing health services as referred to in paragraph (3) shall be regulated by Regent Regulation.

### Paragraph 4

Social Rehabilitation

#### Article 32

1. Social rehabilitation as referred to in Article 26 paragraph (3) letter c is aimed at restoring and developing the capacity of women and children who experience social dysfunction due to criminal and/or civil problems to be able to carry out their social functions in society properly.  
2. Social rehabilitation as referred to in paragraph (1) includes services:a. counseling;  
b. provision of safe houses; andc. spiritual guidance.  
3. In implementing the services as referred to in paragraph (2), UPTD PPA may cooperate with women crisis centres and/or existing social community organizations.

#### Article 33

1. Counseling services as referred to in Article 32 paragraph (2) letter a aim to:a. help women and Children victims of acts of violence recognize their problems and find effective ways to overcome them themselves;  
b. empower women and children who experience criminal and/or civil problems to determine their future;  
c. provide moral support to women and children who experience criminal and/or civil problems in facing the process they are undergoing; andd. make women and children who experience criminal and/or civil problems feel accepted in the community environment.  
2. Counseling services are carried out by gender-responsive social rehabilitation officers, consisting of:a. counselors;  
b. psychologists; and/orc. psychiatrists.  
3. Counseling services are carried out in a place that guarantees a sense of security, comfort and confidentiality of information from women victims of acts of violence.  
4. The stages of counseling services as referred to in paragraph (3) include:a. initial counseling;  
b. follow-up counseling;  
c. case discussion; andd. site visits.

#### Article 34

1. The provision of safe houses as referred to in Article 32 paragraph (2) letter b may be provided by the Regional Government for women and children who experience criminal and/or civil problems.  
2. The provision of safe house services as referred to in paragraph (1) aims to protect women and children as victims of criminal and/or civil problems from intimidation by perpetrators or other parties.  
3. The location of safe houses as referred to in paragraph (1) must be kept confidential.  
4. The criteria for women and children as victims of criminal and/or civil problems who require safe houses include:a. their lives are threatened;  
b. receiving rejection from family or community; c. requiring intensive services but their residence is relatively far away; and/ord. abandoned if not placed in a safe house.

#### Article 35

1. Spiritual guidance as referred to in Article 32 paragraph (2) letter c aims at psychological recovery. (2) Spiritual guidance as referred to in paragraph (1) is implemented based on the principles of:a. not imposing any religion or belief; andb. leaving the final decision in the hands of the victim (self-determination).  
3. Spiritual guidance is carried out by spiritual guidance officers.

### Paragraph 5

Assistance

#### Article 36

1. Assistance services as referred to in Article 26 paragraph (3) letter d are carried out for the strengthening and recovery of victims of violence.  
2. In providing assistance services as referred to in paragraph (1), P2TP2A may cooperate with persons or institutions that have expertise to conduct counseling, therapy and advocacy.

